Some added functionality to Curt McMullen's 'ORBITS OF AUTOMORPHISMS OF K3 SURFACES, Version 3.2 3/2006'
Much of this ReadMe is a regurgitation of his ReadMe.

CODE VERSIONS.
The code corresponding to arXiv v1 is archived at
https://github.com/ethanhcoo/K3entropy/releases/tag/arxiv-v1.
The main branch contains the code for the current version of the paper.

QUICKSTART.
Starting in 'example' or 'src' folders.
To execute './Arcs.run' type
cd ..; cd src; make; cd ..; cd example; ./Arcs.run
Same for other .run files

SOURCE LAYOUT.
The source tree is divided by purpose:

	src/certification/
		The three rigorous manuscript verifiers, their shared certificate
		modules and canonical data, and a few unit tests for the shared
		certification infrastructure.

	src/illustration_and_exploration/
		Plotting and mapping-class programs, nonrigorous exploratory and
		high-precision reference computations, and their K3/PostScript helpers.

The makefile remains in src/, so all build and check commands below are
unchanged.  Generated executables are still written to bin/.

RIGOROUS CERTIFICATION.
From the src directory, run the shared-module tests and the three manuscript
certificates with

	make checkCertificateCommon
	make checkPeriodicOrbitCertification
	make checkDerivativeCertification
	make checkPathCertification

To build and run all of these, use

	make checkAllCertifications

The path certification is the longest and may take several minutes.  The
plain 'make all' target builds the programs but does not run the certificates.

THEORY.  A K3 surface S in R^3 is specified by the equation

(1+x^2)(1+y^2)(1+z^2) + Axyz = B

In many files, we set $A = 10$, $B = 2$. 
	warning: periodicExact, verifyPeriodicOrbit, derivativesExact, derivativesInterval, derivatives, and verifyArcs have $A = 10$, $B = 2$ hard-coded into some functions

Each line parallel to the x, y or z axis meets S in 2 points, so we obtain 3 involutions by "sheet interchange".
These maps preserves a natural volume element on S.

Set f = Iz Iy Ix;

PROGRAMS.  The source files are grouped by purpose below.

RIGOROUS CERTIFICATION PROGRAMS (certification/).

verifyPeriodicOrbit.c
		- Rigorously certifies the Appendix C.3 pseudo-orbit, chart-branch, residual, and table bounds using outward-rounded MPFR intervals
	Inputs:
		- The exact pseudo-orbit coordinates and chart choices stored in k3_orbit_data.c
	Outputs:
		- Prints the certified residual table and full interval enclosures
		- Exits unsuccessfully if any domain, branch, residual, or strict inequality cannot be certified

derivativesInterval.c
		- Rigorously encloses the same derivative information using outward-rounded MPFR interval arithmetic
	Inputs:
		- The exact pseudo-orbit coordinates and chart choices stored in k3_orbit_data.c
	Outputs:
		- Prints interval enclosures for the derivatives and derivative matrices to the command line (stderr)
		- Certifies K < 115, R < 163, M < 442, positivity of the discriminant on the required neighborhoods, and the matrix-entry error bound used in the Arxiv preprint

verifyArcs.c
		- Rigorously certifies the Appendix C.4 path certificate
		- Constructs rational polygonal representatives of the transported relative homotopy classes and computes their arc-data exactly
	Inputs:
		- The exact pseudo-orbit coordinates and chart choices stored in k3_orbit_data.c
		- The expected unreduced and reduced arc-data stored in arc_certificate_data.c
	Outputs:
		- Prints the certified puncture order, exact polygonal arc-data, reduced mclass words, and interval-work statistics
		- Checks rational containment, overlaps, embeddedness, transversality, and agreement with the stored unreduced and reduced rows
		- Exits unsuccessfully if any analytic or exact-geometric test is inconclusive

CERTIFICATION SUPPORT MODULES AND TESTS (certification/).

certificate_interval.c and certificate_interval.h
		- Shared outward-rounded MPFR interval arithmetic for the rigorous certificate programs
		- Each certification program explicitly selects its own precision
		- Operations return status values so the calling certification program can provide certificate-specific failure messages

k3_orbit_data.c and k3_orbit_data.h
		- Store the ten exact pseudo-orbit coordinate strings and chart choices in one canonical table
		- Used by verifyPeriodicOrbit.c, derivativesInterval.c, and verifyArcs.c

arc_certificate_data.c, arc_certificate_data.h,
	arc_word.c, and arc_word.h
		- arc_certificate_data.c stores both the literal unreduced position/height rows checked by verifyArcs.c and the reduced rows used by mclass.c
		- arc_word.c performs the relative word reduction and compares computed words with the stored position/height rows

testCertificateInterval.c, testK3OrbitData.c, and testArcWord.c
		- Test the shared interval-arithmetic functions, the stored orbit and chart data, and the arc-word normalization.

ILLUSTRATION AND EXPLORATION PROGRAMS (illustration_and_exploration/).

periodic.c
		- Experimentation only (No MPFR)
		- Useful for finding recurrent orbits

derivatives.c
		- Experimentation only (No MPFR)
		- Outputs derivative information along an orbit without using GNU MPFR library

arcs.c
		- Useful for generating uncertified plots of image arcs and uncertified arc data
	Inputs in arcs.c:
		- the x,y,z coordinates of a pseudo-periodic point and its period 
	Inputs in command line (edit in arcs.run):
		-b	Suppress plotted arc lines.
		-c [x y] -r [r]
			Set window for image by giving center and "radius".
			Default is center (0,0), radius 1.2.
		-e [eps]
			Set the accurancy of the arcs plot. 
			The arc is drawn as a series of line segments each of length about eps.
		-p [A B]
			Change the parameters for the surface S.
		-q	Currently has no effect; diagnostic output remains enabled.
		-t	Currently has no effect on arc plotting.
		-s 	Rescales to unit sphere
		-u 	Rescales, stereographically projects to the plane, and maps coordinatewise to a stretched rectangle
		-w [i]
			Takes ith iteration under f of each arc p_k, i.e. f^i(p_k)
		-v [m] 
			Plots only f^i(p_m), the image of the mth arc. If this is missing, all arcs are plotted.
	Outputs
		- arc data for $f^i(p_k)$, where $i$ is fixed and $k$ varies over $0\leq k < orbit_length -1
		- PostScript is printed to stdout; arc-data and diagnostics are printed to stderr
	To generate the starting point of 'figure4':
		(i) Make sure that:
				marked_point.x = -1.726895448754858426328854724474;  
				marked_point.y = 1.041643093944314148360673792017;
				marked_point.z = 1.726895448754858426328854724474;	
		(ii) In Arcs.run: 
				../bin/arcs -r 3 -e .002 -p 10 2 -u -w 1| ps2pdf - > figure4.pdf 
				open figure4.pdf &
		(iii) To see each image arc individually, in Arcs.run:
				../bin/arcs -r 3 -e .0002 -p 10 2 -u -v 0  -w 1 | ps2pdf - > arc0.pdf 
				open arc0.pdf &
			and change arcs by changing the integer folling '-v' to anything 0-8

mclass.c
		- Exact downstream computation from the certified arc-data; it is not an interval-certification program
		- used for writing the mapping class of f^2 as a product of dehn half-twists
		Input:
			- Certified reduced arc-data stored in arc_certificate_data.c
		Output:
			- Mapping class of g^{-1}, \overline{g}, and f^2 in terms of dehn half-twists (see Arxiv preprint for notation)

FlipperDilatation.ipynb
		- A jupyter notebook file, for running Flipper inside SageMath.
		- Uses a hard-coded mapping-class word in flipper to find the dilatation of [f^2]

k3.c and k3.h
		- k3.c contains some functions used in other files.
	        - k3.h declares these functions and sets error thresholds.

ps.c
		- Contains PostScript plotting and coordinate-transformation helpers used by the illustration programs.

LEGACY AND REFERENCE PROGRAMS.

illustration_and_exploration/periodicExact.c
		- Legacy high-precision reference, provided certification in earlier version of the paper, superseded for certification by verifyPeriodicOrbit.c
		- Checks whether a particular orbit is a delta-pseudo-orbit for fixed delta, using the GNU MPFR library
	Inputs: All inputs to periodicExact happen within periodicExact.c
		- Specify a_i, b_i (see Arxiv preprint for notation) where 0\leq i < porbit_length
		- Specify delta
	Outputs:
		- Prints 'successful delta pseudo-orbit' to stderr if for all i, d(\phi_i^c(a_i, b_i), (a_{i+1 (mod porbit_length)}, b_{i+1 (mod porbit_length)})) <= delta
		- Prints 'failure' to stderr otherwise; currently exits with status 0 in either case
		Note: Order of operations in function definitions for [alpha], [beta], [p_{\pm}], [f], [f_i^c] agree with order used in the Appendix of Arxiv preprint to produce error estimates.

illustration_and_exploration/derivativesExact.c
		- Legacy high-precision point evaluation, superseded for certification by derivativesInterval.c
		- Gives derivative information for the specific pseudo-orbit above using the GNU MPFR library (see Tables 2 and 3 of Arxiv preprint)
	Inputs: All inputs to derivativesExact happen within derivativesExact.c
		- Specify a_i,b_i
	Outputs:
		- Prints derivatives to command line (stderr)

See the individual .c files for comments describing specific functions

EXAMPLES.

The .run files in example/ can be run using the procedure from QUICKSTART.

An example of a arcs.run is:
	../bin/arcs -r 3 -e .0002 -p 10 2 -u -v 0  -w 1 | ps2pdf - > arc0.pdf 
	open arc0.pdf &


TROUBLESHOOTING

ARCS
	IF: 'path length exceeded N'
	THEN: increase eps, increase MaxPathlength

	IF: 'trouble assigning start/end point'
	THEN: input point is not sufficiently pseudo-periodic
